t it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1.2y-0.002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the number of seals cannot exceed $600.$" 13194139535852 true] [17592186369217 1145 "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,1).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png)" 13194139535852 false] [17592186369217 1145 "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n**Step 