ligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-3y+2z=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\nx-3y+2z=-1\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx=3y-2z-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3y-2z-1 \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y,z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3y-2z-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370269 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370270 13194139535852 true] [17592186370269 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370271 13194139535852 true] [17592186370269 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370272 13194139535852 true] [17592186370269 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370273 13194139535852 true] [17592186370269 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370274 13194139535852 true] [17592186370269 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535852 true] [17592186370269 1041 "selection" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370269 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370274 13194139535852 true] [17592186370269 1110 #uuid "3585bf89-95f0-53a1-98b3-ada8aefa7ab7" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370270 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}1-3y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370270 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535852 true] [17592186370270 1111 #uuid "6e81cea7-9301-5f5e-acd1-f773d3aa1398" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370271 1034 "No solution" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370271 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535852 true] [17592186370271 1111 #uuid "c33ac748-14a7-5f21-904a-e15f3ae5a44c" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370272 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}1-3y+2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370272 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535852 true] [17592186370272 1111 #uuid "0b0e6670-867d-5d48-8aa8-48ab6ebe8ca9" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370273 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}y-2z-3 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370273 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535852 true] [17592186370273 1111 #uuid "fc71e42b-e4ea-5505-ada6-b870ccf8cfd3" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370274 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}3y-2z-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370274 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535852 true] [17592186370274 1111 #uuid "a6acc012-d782-5f85-8952-1217ee796119" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370275 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370276 13194139535852 true] [17592186370275 1089 "q-142950" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370275 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535852 true] [17592186370275 1100 "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535852 true] [17592186370275 1113 #uuid "3f49365e-ca6f-5ad4-88ef-c7c6b1f87da0" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370275 1145 "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)=\\frac{P(X=2,Y=y)}{P(X=2)}=\\frac{f(2,y)}{{f}_{X}(2)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $y\\in {2,4},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=2) & =\\frac{f(2,2)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.2}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(2,4)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.4}{0.6}=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370276 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370277 13194139535852 true] [17592186370276 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370278 13194139535852 true] [17592186370276 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370279 13194139535852 true] [17592186370276 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370280 13194139535852 true] [17592186370276 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370281 13194139535852 true] [17592186370276 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535852 true] [17592186370276 1041 "selection" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370276 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370280 13194139535852 true] [17592186370276 1110 #uuid "ac904a16-7fa1-5eaf-8cdc-55f1811a3a9b" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370277 1034 "| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370277 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535852 true] [17592186370277 1111 #uuid "8aaf1d1d-d612-5e7f-b448-1ea7a1662e0a" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370278 1034 "| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ |" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370278 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535852 true] [17592186370278 1111 #uuid "146f68a5-1a68-5b5d-8ab0-255c85500ce3" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370279 1034 "| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370279 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535852 true] [17592186370279 1111 #uuid "db04c68b-84c4-511c-b823-9ce336d4fe30" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370280 1034 "| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370280 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535852 true] [17592186370280 1111 #uuid "e9419260-4654-5f71-93a5-e67574458ee0" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370281 1034 "| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{5}{6}$ |" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370281 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535852 true] [17592186370281 1111 #uuid "4b9eddca-daa4-5c9e-bb95-4c3f11a05f59" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370282 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370283 13194139535852 true] [17592186370282 1089 "q-157213" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370282 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535852 true] [17592186370282 1100 "Consider the section of the  parabola $3{y}^{2}-4y-16x=0$ that lies in the first quadrant. Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=8$ on the parabola at the point where $x=2,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." 13194139535852 true] [17592186370282 1113 #uuid "de4a72dc-5997-53bc-8d7e-03a96c24c883" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370282 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-4y-16x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{y}^{2}-4y-16x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{y}^{2}-4y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(16x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3{y}^{2}-4y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(16x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6y-4)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=8$ and $x=2.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot (6y-4)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=2$ into the equation of the  parabola. \n\nAt the point where $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-4y-16x & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-4y-16\\cdot (2) & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-4y-32 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)(3y+8) & =0 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y>0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $y=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot (6\\cdot (4)-4)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16 & =0. \\\\ 160-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16 & =0. \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370284 13194139535852 true] [17592186370283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370285 13194139535852 true] [17592186370283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370286 13194139535852 true] [17592186370283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370287 13194139535852 true] [17592186370283 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370288 13194139535852 true] [17592186370283 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535852 true] [17592186370283 1041 "selection" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370283 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370285 13194139535852 true] [17592186370283 1110 #uuid "84a3cd7d-5568-506a-9757-768e163906f0" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370284 1034 "12" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370284 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535852 true] [17592186370284 1111 #uuid "00be453c-bf66-5eeb-9f10-53819fc0610e" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370285 1034 "10" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370285 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535852 true] [17592186370285 1111 #uuid "b7f27ed4-38f6-55b2-9826-3011918fc845" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370286 1034 "8" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370286 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535852 true] [17592186370286 1111 #uuid "950b71dd-052f-5679-a749-dacb9f4bc1d8" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370287 1034 "6" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370287 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535852 true] [17592186370287 1111 #uuid "c63e12a6-4703-5ada-abbf-19a4f989ffdb" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370288 1034 "4" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370288 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535852 true] [17592186370288 1111 #uuid "da21aa11-9e29-5dd5-84f5-4ef9c80a32fc" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370289 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370290 13194139535852 true] [17592186370289 1089 "q-161651" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370289 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535852 true] [17592186370289 1100 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $A=∂A\\cup \\text{int}A$\n- $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n- $∂A⊂A$" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370289 1113 #uuid "b95418a1-1dbc-53a9-8483-916fb7717c78" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370289 1145 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/a39ecae41a88e873fa9eecb30a5b554be9ec98cf630851715ab35f2f873710f2.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statements I and III are false. Since ${C}_{1}⊂∂A-A,$ we must have that $∂A⊄A$ and also that $A\\ne ∂A\\cup \\text{int}A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370291 13194139535852 true] [17592186370290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370292 13194139535852 true] [17592186370290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370293 13194139535852 true] [17592186370290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370294 13194139535852 true] [17592186370290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370295 13194139535852 true] [17592186370290 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535852 true] [17592186370290 1041 "selection" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370290 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370295 13194139535852 true] [17592186370290 1110 #uuid "fec4bcbc-4d0f-5f8c-ba81-7332f3f03847" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370291 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370291 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535852 true] [17592186370291 1111 #uuid "feedb588-a2b7-5701-8b34-90c63ed3a7ec" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370292 1034 "I only" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370292 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535852 true] [17592186370292 1111 #uuid "c3346293-ff33-58e7-af95-c78afe331557" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370293 1034 "III only" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370293 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535852 true] [17592186370293 1111 #uuid "364998de-1b33-59e8-8e87-1eacea9408c5" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370294 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370294 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535852 true] [17592186370294 1111 #uuid "0da0c582-7df0-51a7-9b1f-8c160d6a5c35" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370295 1034 "II only" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370295 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535852 true] [175921