hat a full charge lasts for $36$ hours or more? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ |" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370382 1113 #uuid "a6e27650-4b1b-5548-99e3-f1ed5cca0feb" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370382 1145 "Let $X$ represent the time the full charge lasts. Then we have $X∼N(32,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X\\ge 36).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 36) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{36-32}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 1) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(1)=1-0.8413≈0.1587.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a full charge lasts $36$ hours or more is approximately $0.16.$" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370383 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370384 13194139535852 true] [17592186370383 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370385 13194139535852 true] [17592186370383 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370386 13194139535852 true] [17592186370383 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370387 13194139535852 true] [17592186370383 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370388 13194139535852 true] [17592186370383 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535852 true] [17592186370383 1041 "selection" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370383 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370387 13194139535852 true] [17592186370383 1110 #uuid "7361dbca-3d56-533f-971e-e8e60313830e" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370384 1034 "0.27" 13194139535852 true] [17592186370384 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535852 true] [17592186370384 1111 #uuid "ff27dc57-e368-5eed-bd0c-ec2c96ad0e37" 1319413953585